---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const jsonLd = [{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "CreativeWork", "name": "Iris growth pipeline", "description": "Automated marketing for the Iris app. AI infographic posters gated by a written creative contract, published daily to Instagram and Pinterest.", "author": {"@type": "Person", "name": "Arbaz Siddiqui", "url": "https://www.arbazsiddiqui.me"}, "url": "https://www.arbazsiddiqui.me/iris-pipeline/"}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.arbazsiddiqui.me/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Projects", "item": "https://www.arbazsiddiqui.me/projects/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Iris growth pipeline", "item": "https://www.arbazsiddiqui.me/iris-pipeline/"}]}];
---

<Base
  title="Iris: Autonomous Growth Pipeline"
  description="An autonomous growth pipeline that creates quality-gated infographics, publishes daily to Instagram and Pinterest, and localizes App Store content in 11 languages."
  ogImage="/og/iris-pipeline.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="The Iris growth pipeline, automated poster generation and publishing."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="narrow section">
    <p class="kicker">Growth pipeline · live</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">The Iris growth pipeline</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <a href="/iris">Iris</a> is a solo app competing against period trackers
      with marketing teams. So its marketing is also software: a pipeline
      that designs, checks, and publishes the app's content every day
      without me.
    </p>

    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>Publishes</dt><dd>Instagram Reels, Stories, and carousels, Pinterest pins, on daily slots</dd>
      <dt>Creates</dt><dd>Infographic posters on women's-health topics, generated by an image model that owns all the typography</dd>
      <dt>Gate</dt><dd>Every poster passes a written creative contract before it can ship</dd>
      <dt>Store listing</dt><dd>App Store screenshots and metadata localized to eleven languages, produced by a second pipeline</dd>
    </dl>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The creative contract</h2>
      <p>
        The interesting part is not generating images; it is refusing bad
        ones. Every poster is checked against a written contract: at most 95
        words, four to six information modules, all content inside
        mobile-safe geometry, reviewed at phone size before approval. A
        diversity ledger tracks topic, layout family, palette, and cast
        across everything published, so no two posts repeat a look. Posters
        that pass wait in a reviewed queue; publishing itself is unattended.
      </p>
      <h2>Distribution</h2>
      <p>
        Approved posters ship as seven-second static Reels (audio picked at
        publish time), the first slide goes out as a Story with App Store
        stickers, and the same artwork is repackaged as Pinterest pins on a
        women's-health board. Receipt ledgers make every step idempotent:
        a crashed run resumes, and nothing posts twice.
      </p>
      <h2>Reception</h2>
      <p>
        The channel started from nothing and is running at about 100K views
        a month, every post published unattended. Instagram and Pinterest
        both carry App Store links, so that reach is the top of the funnel
        for <a href="/iris">the app</a> and it costs nothing to run. The idea
        was to automate not just the development but the distribution too, and
        the same machinery drives <a href="/footnote">The Footnote</a> at 500K
        monthly views.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-title { margin-block: 0.35rem 0.8rem; }
  .lede { margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
</style>
